---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Measure the value AI creates, not the time it saves — Field notes"
  description="Time saved is a fine first measure for AI. It is not the value. A note on what I would put at the top of the pyramid now."
>
  <SiteHeader solid />

  <main id="main" class="page">
    <article class="shell note-article">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href={`${base}field-notes/`}>Field notes</a> · Nov 2026</p>
      <h1>Measure the value AI creates, not the time it saves</h1>
      <p class="page-lede">
        I once built a measurement system with time saved because of AI at the top. Today I wouldn't put it there again.
      </p>

      <div class="prose-block">
        <p>
          This note started with an episode of the Engineering Enablement podcast:
          <a href="https://getdx.com/podcast/inside-the-devex-metrics-compass-with-andre-meyer/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Inside the DevEx Metrics Compass with André Meyer</a>.
          André maps more than 120 developer experience metrics, and one of his points stuck with me: AI usage, acceptance
          rates and pull request counts show activity, not value. I agree. Measuring real value is hard, and we should do
          it anyway.
        </p>
        <p>
          André looks at developer experience. My work sits next to it: realized value for business, R&amp;D and product
          innovation people. For me the common denominator is value created versus faster production.
        </p>

        <h2>The project</h2>
        <p>
          A client rolled out a RAG-based enterprise search that indexed knowledge from more than five dozen data
          sources. It was the first step of their agentic vision. Start with information retrieval, then bring agents
          into budgeting and new product creation workflows.
        </p>
        <p>
          They wanted to measure time saved for their R&amp;D and other knowledge-work intensive, product-related
          departments. The baseline was a time-savings potential of about 3 hours per employee per week. After go-live
          came repeated benefit measurements, around one value question: how does the tool help me refocus my work, and
          where does it support me day to day?
        </p>
        <p>We built the measurement as a pyramid:</p>
        <div class="prose-table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Level</th>
                <th>What we measured</th>
                <th>Data source</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>1. North star</td>
                <td>Total benefits, as time saved</td>
                <td>Sum of the five primary KPIs</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>2. Five primary KPIs</td>
                <td>
                  Data discovery and access, qualitative analysis, populating templates, ad-hoc result inquiries,
                  compliance monitoring
                </td>
                <td>Quarterly survey</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>2.5. Adoption indicators</td>
                <td>
                  Weekly unique users, active sessions, 24-hour return rate, power user progression, thumbs up or down,
                  response relevancy
                </td>
                <td>Usage statistics and application traces</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>3. Technical health</td>
                <td>Hallucination spikes, query response time and more</td>
                <td>Azure plus custom technical data points and traces</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The quarterly survey mixed quantitative and qualitative questions. Six sections fed the five KPIs and overall
          perceived value. Time saved came from two sources: usage statistics and self-reports.
        </p>

        <h2>Where I pushed</h2>
        <p>
          Time saved is fine, but I told the client the more important question is what that saved time turns into.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>Freed-up time only matters if it creates something.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          It felt scary for me. Measuring what people do with saved time felt like a direct attack on their
          productivity, a way to squeeze more out of them for the company. So the project didn't measure it
          quantitatively. They added one subjective question to the quarterly survey.
        </p>

        <h2>What I'd do today</h2>
        <p>Looking back, the layered approach was right, but too complex to start with. And the top of the pyramid was wrong.</p>
        <p>
          Today I still do not recommend time saved as the north star. It treats AI as a way to squeeze more out of
          people, and it misses what AI is meant to help us create: new ideas, better and more frequent decisions,
          products that customers love, more stable platforms, and net new business.
        </p>
        <p>This is how I'd set it up now:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>North star:</strong> additional value created, or a close proxy. For an R&amp;D team, that could be a
            new product variant tested successfully, brought to market with additional revenue created. And most
            importantly: the north star should be aligned with the strategic goals.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Leading indicators</strong> that show up weeks before the north star moves. For example, a clear
            convergence of observations or opinions in a customer focus group testing a new variant, and maybe even more
            rapid iterations of synthetic focus groups. Say, as a made-up example, testers clearly agreeing they like a
            new strawberry-icy-lime flavor.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Telemetry and surveys</strong> underneath, to triangulate. Telemetry and usage statistics tell you
            what changed, surveys help explain why. André makes exactly the same point, but for developer experience.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>Being faster is a fine first step. It just isn't the value.</p>
        <p class="aside">Project details have been anonymized to protect privacy and confidentiality.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="note-back">
        <a href={`${base}field-notes/`}>← All field notes</a>
        ·
        <a href={`${base}how-i-work/`}>How I work</a>
        ·
        <a href={`${base}use-cases/`}>Use cases</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
